  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Winter oysterling (Scytinotus ringens) is a small, delicate fungus belonging to the Porotheleaceae family, characterized by its pale, oyster-like fruiting bodies. It is a cold-weather specialist found fruiting during the winter months in New England and other temperate regions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gaping mouth-like opening",
      "description": "The distinctive feature is the wide, gaping aperture at the apex of the fruiting body, resembling an open mouth, which is reflected in its species name 'ringens'",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pale coloration",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are typically whitish, cream-colored, or pale gray, remaining relatively inconspicuous in winter leaf litter"
    },
    {
      "label": "Small size",
      "description": "The fruiting bodies are quite diminutive, usually only a few millimeters in diameter, requiring close inspection to observe properly"
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Winter oysterling frupts on decaying hardwood logs and branches in moist forests during winter months when other fungi are inactive.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its small size and inconspicuous appearance, Scytinotus ringens is one of the few fungi actively fruiting during harsh New England winters, making it a prized find for dedicated winter mushroom hunters.",
